<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">I Saw Three Ships</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">earliest printed version 17th century; modern words published <a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as I Saw Three Ships (Come Sailing In)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;I Saw Three Ships (Come Sailing In)&quot; is an English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a>, number 700 in the Roud Folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Index. The earliest printed version is from the 17th century, possibly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>. The song was probably known traditionally as &quot;As I Sat On a Sunny Bank&quot;, and it was particularly popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>&quot;I Saw Three Ships (Come Sailing In)&quot; is listed as number 700 in the Roud Folk Song Index. Its earliest printed version is from the 17th century, possibly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>. It was probably known traditionally as &quot;As I Sat On a Sunny Bank&quot; and was particularly popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>. The modern text is the nine-verse version published in 1833 by the English lawyer and antiquarian William Sandys.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Each verse repeats its lines, with the refrain &quot;On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> day in the morning&quot;. The ships carry Christ and his mother, sail into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, and bring bells that ring on earth, angels that sing in heaven and souls that sing on earth, before the singers are asked to rejoice. The words place ships at Bethlehem, though the nearest water is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a>, about 32 kilometres away.</p><p>The three ships are thought to come from the three ships that bore the supposed relics of the Biblical Magi to Cologne Cathedral in the 12th century. Another suggestion is the coat of arms of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a>, which showed three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> on blue, and a third is that the ships are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, sometimes called ships of the desert. A 19th-century variant, &quot;I saw three ships come sailing by on New Year's Day&quot;, has three girls on board entertaining at a wedding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Many traditional versions with different melodies have been collected. In the 1910s Cecil Sharp and Janet Blunt noted dozens in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and in the early 1930s James Madison Carpenter recorded several, some in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>. Peter Kennedy recorded John Thomas singing it at Camborne in 1956.</p><p>Jean Ritchie sang a version learned from her Kentucky family, recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-lomax">Alan Lomax</a> in 1949, and in 1952 she and George Pickow heard Elizabeth Cronin sing &quot;The Bells of Heaven&quot; at Macroom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-cork">County Cork</a>. Arrangements include those of Martin Shaw in The Oxford Book of Carols and of David Willcocks and John Rutter in Carols for Choirs. Among the later recordings are those of Nat King Cole in 1960, Jon Anderson in 1985, Sting in 1997 and Cyndi Lauper in 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “I Saw Three Ships” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
